  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nThe wet pattern that the Northland has been experiencing for the \npast few days is expected to come to an end today as low pressure \npropagates downstream. Latest sfc analysis places a low pressure \ncenter over central Lake Superior. A trowal associated with this \nvertically-stacked low is currently over portions of the CWA, \nresulting in a broad area of rain along the North Shore and in \nnorthwest Wisconsin. A 500mb shortwave trough is expected to dive \nsouth-southeast from far northern Ontario into the Upper Great Lakes \nregion this morning into the afternoon, which will cause this low \npressure to finally propagate downstream. Expect rain to exit the \nCWA by this afternoon.  \n\nIn addition to propagating the low pressure downstream, this \narriving shortwave trough will be bringing a colder airmass into the \nregion. 850mb temps by this evening over western Lake Superior are \nforecast to be around -6 to -7 degC. When combined with steep lapse \nrates in excess of 8 degC/km below 800mb, lake sfc temps around 9 \ndegC, and north-northeast winds, lake effect snow is forecast in the \nsnowbelts of north-central WI this evening into tonight. The RRFS \nand REFS indicate the potential for up to 1\" of accumulation, \nalthough amounts will likely be minimal due to temps remaining above \nfreezing through the daytime hours today. Any light snow \naccumulation will most likely be on grassy and elevated surfaces, \nquickly melting tomorrow as high temps warm into the 40s. \n\nIn sharp contrast to the wet weather we've been seeing, a much drier \npattern is expected to settle over the CWA starting on Friday and \npersist through at least early next week. Global models largely keep \nthe storm track north of the International Border, although a \nshortwave is anticipated to move through the region on Saturday. \nMoisture associated with this Clipper-track shortwave is progged to \nbe fairly minimal, with the main impact being increased cloud cover \non Saturday as a dry front sweeps through. Temperatures on Friday \nthrough the remainder of the forecast period will generally be above-\nnormal. For context, normal high temps for this time of year are \naround the upper 30s to low 40s with overnight lows in the 20s.",
